menu "PlantHub Claiming"

config PLANTHUB_FACTORY_RESET_GPIO
    int "GPIO for the 10-second factory-reset hold"
    default 9 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6
    default 28 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
    default 0
    help
        Pin that must be held low for 10 seconds to clear the claim key,
        WiFi credentials and swarm role/pairing. This is the documented
        last-resort recovery for a device that cannot be reached any
        other way -- a locked-out hub, or a node that runs no web server
        at all because it is paired (see PlanV1 section 8e/8f) -- so it
        has to match the BOOT button of the board actually in use.

        The default follows the target's own BOOT button: GPIO9 on
        ESP32-C6 devkits, GPIO28 on ESP32-C5 ones. The trailing GPIO0 is
        only Kconfig's required terminal default for an unlisted target;
        no supported board uses it.

        Getting this wrong is not cosmetic. This was once hardcoded to
        GPIO0, and it was found on hardware that no amount of holding
        BOOT reset an ESP32-C3 node -- GPIO0 is simply not wired to a
        button there, so the only documented recovery path did nothing at
        all on what was then the primary target. That chip was dropped in
        M6a; the lesson was not, which is why the C6 value above was
        verified on hardware instead of taken from a datasheet. At M6a's
        gate a 10-second hold on GPIO9 of an ESP32-C6-DevKitC-1 cleared
        the claim key and the WiFi credentials and dropped the hub back to
        AP mode, while its 3 plants and 1 rule survived -- the documented
        contract exactly. Any new board still has to be checked the same
        way before a release ships; that is the part the C3 incident
        actually taught, and it does not expire because one board passed.
        Change this only for a custom board whose button is on some other
        pin.

endmenu
